### 61. Section. Conditional Early Release from Punishment

(1)	A person who has been sentenced to deprivation of liberty, except for short-term deprivation of liberty, may be conditionally released early from serving the principal punishment if there is reason to believe that after release they will be able to integrate into society without committing a criminal offence.

(2)	Taking into account the convicted person's personality and behaviour during the serving of the punishment, conditional early release from punishment may be determined if:

1)	the convicted person has achieved a certain result of resocialisation;

2)	the convicted person has, according to their possibilities, voluntarily compensated for the harm caused by the criminal offence;

3)	the convicted person has the possibility after release to obtain means of subsistence by lawful means;

4)	the time period specified in the law regulating the execution of criminal punishments has passed since the imposition of punishment for violation of the punishment serving regime and there are no valid punishments for administrative offences committed during the execution of the deprivation of liberty punishment;

5)	the convicted person is addressing and is ready to continue addressing their psychological problems that caused or could cause the commission of a criminal offence;

6)	the convicted person has consented to treatment for alcohol, narcotic, psychotropic or toxic substance addiction if the criminal offence was committed due to alcohol, narcotic, psychotropic or toxic substance addiction.

(21) When conditionally releasing early from punishment, electronic monitoring may be applied to the convicted person, observing the following conditions:

1)	the convicted person consents to electronic monitoring;

2)	the implementation of electronic monitoring is possible at the convicted person's place of residence;

3)	the application of electronic monitoring will promote the convicted person's integration into society.

(3)	Conditional early release from punishment may be initiated when the convicted person has actually served:

1)	not less than half of the imposed punishment for a less serious crime committed;

2)	not less than two-thirds of the imposed punishment if it was imposed for a serious crime, as well as if the convicted person is a person who has previously been sentenced to deprivation of liberty for an intentional crime and the criminal record for this crime has not been removed or expunged;

3)	not less than three-quarters of the imposed punishment if it was imposed for a particularly serious crime, as well as if the convicted person is a person who has previously been conditionally released early from punishment and has committed a new intentional crime during the unserved part of the punishment;

4)	twenty-five years of the deprivation of liberty punishment if the convicted person is a person who has been sentenced to life imprisonment.

(31)	If, in accordance with the provisions of Section 2.1 of this Article, the application of electronic monitoring is possible, conditional early release from punishment with the imposition of electronic monitoring may be initiated when the convicted person has actually served:

1)	not less than one-third of the imposed punishment for a less serious crime;

2)	not less than half of the imposed punishment if it was imposed for a serious crime, as well as if the convicted person is a person who has previously been sentenced to deprivation of liberty for an intentional crime and the criminal record for this crime has not been removed or expunged;

3)	not less than two-thirds of the imposed punishment if it was imposed for a particularly serious crime, as well as if the convicted person is a person who has previously been conditionally released early from punishment and has committed a new intentional crime during the unserved part of the punishment;

4)	not less than twenty-four years of the deprivation of liberty punishment if the convicted person is a person who has been sentenced to life imprisonment.

(4)	A person conditionally released early shall, during the unserved part of the punishment, fulfil the obligations provided for in the law regulating the execution of criminal punishments and established by the punishment execution institution. If the person conditionally released early fails to fulfil the said obligations without justifiable reason, the court, based on an application from the punishment execution institution, may adopt a decision on the execution of the unserved part of the punishment.

(41)	If a person conditionally released early, to whom electronic monitoring has been applied, fails without justifiable reason to fulfil the obligations related to electronic monitoring specified in the law regulating the execution of criminal punishments, revokes their consent to electronic monitoring, or if in the circumstances in which they live the implementation of electronic monitoring is no longer possible, the court, based on an application from the punishment execution institution, shall adopt a decision on the execution of the unserved part of the punishment.

(42)	If a person conditionally released early, to whom electronic monitoring has been applied, has exemplarily fulfilled the obligations provided for in the law regulating the execution of criminal punishments and established by the punishment execution institution and the time period specified in Section 61, Paragraph three of this Law has occurred, according to which conditional early release from punishment without the imposition of electronic monitoring is possible, the court, based on an application from the punishment execution institution, may adopt a decision on the cancellation of electronic monitoring. If the court adopts a decision to cancel electronic monitoring, the convicted person shall be supervised in accordance with the supervision rules for persons conditionally released early from serving punishment provided for in the regulatory enactments regulating the execution of criminal punishments.

(43)	A person sentenced to life imprisonment who has been released from serving the punishment conditionally early shall, after release, be under the supervision of the punishment execution institution for life and shall fulfil the obligations provided for in the law regulating the execution of criminal punishments and established by the punishment execution institution. If a person sentenced to life imprisonment who has been released from serving the punishment conditionally early fails without justifiable reason to fulfil the obligations provided for in the law regulating the execution of criminal punishments and established by the punishment execution institution, the court, based on an application from the punishment execution institution, may adopt a decision on the replacement of the early supervision period with deprivation of liberty for life (life imprisonment).

(44)	If a person sentenced to life imprisonment who has been released from serving the punishment conditionally early and to whom electronic monitoring has been applied fails without justifiable reason to fulfil the obligations related to electronic monitoring specified in the law regulating the execution of criminal punishments, revokes their consent to electronic monitoring, or if in the circumstances in which they live the implementation of electronic monitoring is no longer possible, the court, based on an application from the punishment execution institution, shall adopt a decision on the replacement of the early supervision period with deprivation of liberty for life (life imprisonment).

(5)	If a person for whom conditional early release was determined commits a new criminal offence during the unserved part of the punishment, the court shall impose a punishment on them in accordance with the provisions set out in Sections 51 and 52 of this Law.

(6)	Conditional early release from punishment shall not be applied if it was imposed on an adult for a particularly serious crime committed against a person who has not reached the age of sixteen and is related to sexual violence.

(Ar grozījumiem, kas izdarīti ar 27.05.2004., 21.06.2007., 30.10.2008., 08.07.2011., 01.12.2011., 13.12.2012.,
15.05.2014., 16.10.2014., 11.06.2020. un 17.12.2020. likumu, kas stājas spēkā 01.01.2022.)

61. 1 Section. Electronic Monitoring

(1)	Electronic monitoring is intensive control measures determined by the court to restrict the convicted person's free movement. To implement electronic monitoring, an electronic device is attached to the convicted person's body, which allows control of their presence at a specified place and time.

(2)	The court shall determine electronic monitoring for a period from one month to twelve months.

(3)	The term of electronic monitoring begins on the day when the electronic device is attached to the convicted person's body. Upon expiry of the term of electronic monitoring determined by the court, the convicted person shall be supervised in accordance with the supervision rules for persons conditionally released early from serving punishment provided for in the regulatory enactments regulating the execution of criminal punishments, if the convicted person's unserved punishment period has not yet ended.
(16.10.2014. likuma redakcijā, kas stājas spēkā 01.02.2015. Pants stājas spēkā 01.07.2015. Sk. pārejas noteikumu 19.punktu)

