### 62. Section. Limitation Period for Execution of a Conviction Judgment

(1) A conviction judgment and a prosecutor's order on punishment shall not be enforceable if, from the day it entered into legal force, its execution has not been commenced within the following time limits:

1) two years — if short-term deprivation of liberty, community service or a fine has been imposed;

11) two years after serving the deprivation of liberty sentence — if the sentence — community service — is enforceable independently in the cases provided for in Section 52, Paragraph 2.1 of this Law;

2) (deleted by the Law of 06.06.2019);

21) three years — if probation supervision has been imposed;

3) five years — if deprivation of liberty for a period not exceeding five years has been imposed;

4) ten years — if deprivation of liberty for a period not exceeding ten years has been imposed;

5) fifteen years — if a more severe sentence than deprivation of liberty for ten years has been imposed.

(2) The limitation period shall be interrupted if the convicted person evades serving the sentence or, before the expiry of the limitation period, commits a new criminal offence for which the court has imposed deprivation of liberty for a period not less than one year. If a new criminal offence has been committed, the limitation period shall be counted from the moment of its commission, but if the convicted person has evaded serving the sentence — from the moment when he or she arrives to serve the sentence or from the moment when the convicted person who has been in hiding is detained. However, the conviction judgment shall not be enforceable if fifteen years have passed since it entered into force and the limitation period has not been interrupted by a new criminal offence. The question of whether the convicted person has evaded serving the sentence shall be decided by the court.

(3) The question of the limitation period for a person sentenced to life imprisonment shall be decided by the court.
(With amendments made by the Laws of 12.02.2004, 13.12.2007, 01.12.2011, 13.12.2012, 14.03.2013, 25.09.2014,
29.10.2015, 06.06.2019 and 17.12.2020, which enters into force on 01.01.2022.)

### 63. Section. Expungement and Removal of Criminal Record

(1) A criminal record is the legal consequence of conviction or imposition of a sentence on a person who has committed a criminal offence, which is in force during the period of serving the sentence determined in the court judgment or prosecutor's order on punishment, as well as thereafter until the expungement or removal of the criminal record in the manner prescribed by law.

(2) A person shall be considered convicted from the moment the conviction judgment enters into force or from the day the prosecutor's order on punishment enters into force.

(3) The following shall be recognised as not having a criminal record:

1) persons who have been released from punishment by a court judgment or acquitted;

2) persons for whom the limitation period for execution of a conviction judgment provided for by law has occurred;

3) conditionally convicted persons — one year after the end of the probationary period, but in the case of imposition of an additional sentence
— one year after the end of the period of serving the additional sentence. If the imposed sentence is executed for a conditionally convicted person, the term for expungement of the criminal record shall be calculated taking into account the sentence actually served;

4) after one year — persons who have served short-term deprivation of liberty, community service or a fine;

5) after two years — persons who have served a sentence of deprivation of liberty not exceeding three years, or probation supervision;

6) after five years — persons who have served a sentence of deprivation of liberty exceeding three years but not exceeding five years;

7) after eight years — persons who have served a sentence of deprivation of liberty exceeding five years but not exceeding ten years;

8) after ten years — persons who have served a sentence of deprivation of liberty exceeding ten years.

(4) The term for expungement of a criminal record shall be counted from the day the person has fully served the principal sentence and the additional sentence. The execution of the additional sentence — confiscation of property — shall not affect the counting of the term for expungement of the criminal record.

(5) If a person has been released from punishment before the term in the manner prescribed by law, the term for expungement of the criminal record shall be counted from the day he or she was released from serving the sentence, taking into account the period of actual serving of the sentence.

(6) (Deleted by the Law of 13.12.2012)

(7) If a person convicted with deprivation of liberty has, after serving the sentence, demonstrated his or her reform through exemplary conduct and honest attitude towards work, the court may remove the criminal record before the expiry of the terms specified in this Section.

(8) A criminal record may be removed by way of pardon or amnesty.

(9) Expungement and removal of a criminal record shall annul all criminal law consequences of the criminal offence committed, except those provided for by law if a new criminal offence has been committed before the expungement or removal of the criminal record.
(With amendments made by the Laws of 17.10.2002, 12.02.2004, 28.09.2005, 08.11.2007, 21.10.2010, 13.12.2012,
15.05.2014, 25.09.2014, 10.03.2016, 08.06.2017 and 17.12.2020, which enters into force on 01.01.2022.)

Chapter VII
Specific Features of Criminal Liability of a Minor
(Chapter title amended by the Law of 17.12.2020, which enters into force on 01.01.2022.)

### 64. Section. Liability of a Minor

(1) The provisions of this Chapter shall apply to a person who has not reached the age of eighteen before committing a criminal offence.

(2) Other provisions of the General Part of this Law shall apply to a minor unless otherwise provided for in this Chapter.

(3) Taking into account the objective of punishment specified in the second paragraph of Section 35 of this Law, the primary objective in relation to a minor is his or her resocialisation.
(In the wording of the Law of 17.12.2020, which enters into force on 01.01.2022.)

### 65. Section. Types of Punishment for a Minor

(1) One of the following principal sentences may be imposed on a minor:

1) deprivation of liberty;

2) probation supervision;

3) community service;

4) fine.

(2) The following additional sentences may be imposed on a minor without a principal sentence:

1) probation supervision;

2) restriction of rights;

3) expulsion from the Republic of Latvia.

(3) A prosecutor, when drawing up an order on punishment, may impose probation supervision, community service or a fine, as well as an additional sentence — restriction of rights.
(In the wording of the Law of 17.12.2020, which enters into force on 01.01.2022.)
